= Operating system kernel — Technical =
<onlyinclude>An '''operating system kernel''' implements privileged mechanisms for execution, address-space management, interrupt handling and controlled access to hardware. Its precise trusted-computing-base boundary depends on architecture and deployment.</onlyinclude>

== Scope and kernel responsibility taxonomy ==
The kernel mediates processor scheduling, physical and virtual memory, system-call entry, interrupts and device access, and privilege boundaries. The distribution of these mechanisms among privileged code and user-space services differs by design; a hybrid architecture is not captured by a strict monolithic/microkernel binary. The kernel is not synonymous with the entire operating system. Security is end-to-end and depends additionally on firmware, hardware, privileged drivers, user-space components and configuration.

== Privilege transition and system calls ==
On architectures with separate privilege levels, a system call transfers control through a defined entry mechanism to privileged code. The kernel must interpret arguments, check authorization, validate user-supplied pointers and avoid trusting user-controlled data. A valid pointer-range check alone does not guarantee that subsequent memory access succeeds: the Linux kernel documents that <code>access_ok</code> may succeed while the eventual access returns <code>-EFAULT</code>.<ref name="linuxmm">Linux kernel documentation, [https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/core-api/mm-api.html Memory Management APIs], accessed 2026-11-21.</ref> This is a Linux implementation example, not a universal ABI contract.

== Scheduling and execution contexts ==
A scheduler chooses among runnable tasks subject to policy and available CPUs. Preemption, blocking, priority, affinity and interrupt activity complicate latency. A context switch can entail register and address-space state changes, but its cost is architecture- and workload-dependent. Interrupt handlers execute under constraints different from ordinary process context; implementations may defer work to schedulable contexts. No single scheduling policy guarantees both low worst-case latency and maximal throughput.

== Virtual memory and isolation ==
Kernel memory management coordinates page tables, allocation, mapping and page faults. Page-table permissions and privilege checks can isolate address spaces when hardware and configuration enforce them. A virtual address is not intrinsically a physical address, and mappings may change over time. Isolation is conditional on correctness of the kernel, hardware, privileged extensions and surrounding trusted components.

== Engineering contracts and implementation verification ==
A kernel's system-call ABI must define argument encoding, validation, error reporting, object lifetime and observable concurrency. The design must distinguish process context, hard/soft interrupt contexts, and deferred execution. Scheduling claims require an explicit task model (periodic, sporadic or best effort), processor count, preemption and interrupt assumptions; average utilization alone does not prove schedulability. Virtual-memory correctness requires explicit page-table permission, translation, fault and invalidation rules; stale translation caches and device DMA introduce additional synchronization and isolation concerns. A device driver is a distinct interface component and may run inside or outside the privileged kernel depending on the design.

For reproducible evaluation, specify hardware and privilege architecture, kernel revision, configuration, compiler, scheduler policy, task mix, interrupt load and measurement clocks. Measure syscall and context-switch latency distributions (including tails), fault handling, IPC throughput and isolation failures separately. Comparison between architectures must control for workload and implementation quality; kernel taxonomy alone cannot establish universal performance or security rankings.

== Kernel architecture ==
A monolithic design keeps a relatively broad set of mechanisms, including many drivers, in kernel privilege; a microkernel restricts the privileged core and relies more on interprocess communication with external services. Design names do not determine measurable overhead: message-transfer costs, caching, driver boundaries and workload dominate comparisons.

== Proof obligations and research gaps ==
A proof of functional refinement, a proof of access-control enforcement, and a proof of information-flow noninterference are distinct claims. Their assumptions, system boundary and excluded channels must be documented separately. Relevant open engineering problems include hardware side channels, DMA/IOMMU integration, device-driver trusted code, dynamic reconfiguration, verified toolchains and maintaining proofs as implementations evolve. These are research directions, not claims that any given kernel resolves them.

== Formal assurance and scope ==
Klein and colleagues presented a machine-checked functional-correctness proof for the seL4 microkernel relative to an abstract specification, explicitly assuming correctness of compiler, assembly, boot code and hardware.<ref name="klein">Klein, G. et al. (2009), [https://doi.org/10.1145/1629575.1629596 ''seL4: Formal Verification of an OS Kernel''], SOSP, 207–220.</ref> Such a proof does not establish that all deployments are secure or that every desired security property follows from functional refinement. Later information-flow work specified further conditions for isolation and identified covert-channel limitations.<ref name="murray">Murray, T. et al. (2013), [https://doi.org/10.1109/SP.2013.35 ''seL4: From General Purpose to a Proof of Information Flow Enforcement''], IEEE S&P.</ref>

== Platform-specific interface evidence ==
Linux documentation for the user-access API specifies that a successful <code>access_ok</code> check does not guarantee that a later memory access succeeds; access may still fail with <code>-EFAULT</code>. This behavior is documented in versioned Linux memory-management API references, and exact API wording changes between releases. A generic kernel description should not imply that every architecture implements Linux's ABI.<ref name="linuxaccess">Linux kernel, [https://docs.kernel.org/5.19/core-api/mm-api.html Memory Management APIs (v5.19)], section on user-space memory access.</ref>

== Worked model: scheduling utilization ==
For a hypothetical single processor, suppose two CPU-bound tasks each receive 4 ms of execution in a repeating 10 ms observation window, with 2 ms consumed by other activity or idle time. Their combined observed execution fraction is (4+4)/10 = 0.8, or 80%. This arithmetic does '''not''' predict scheduling fairness, deadlines, context-switch overhead, or the feasibility of any real workload; it merely illustrates an accounting ratio. Time fractions must be measured on a consistent clock and distinguish idle from overhead.

== Numerical model limitations ==
For the illustrative 10 ms interval, two tasks consume 4 ms each: total task CPU utilization is <math>U=(4+4)/10=0.8</math>. The remaining 2 ms may be idle or other execution; this observation does not imply any particular response-time bound or real-time schedulability. For periodic tasks with deadlines, analysis additionally needs task periods, deadlines, blocking, interference and scheduling policy. This is arithmetic illustration, not a measured kernel benchmark.

== Provenance and limitations ==
The Linux memory-management API is primary implementation documentation; it cannot establish cross-platform semantics. The seL4 papers are primary research on a particular kernel and proof model; generalization to Linux, Windows or all microkernels would be unwarranted. Hardware privilege, side channels and the total trusted computing base require separate analysis.

